{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c3d4a41\" lines=\"15-28\">\n<description>\nFit a grey bar into a grey frame by aligning projections with colored columns, then stack colors above.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a grey structural color forming two components: a large rectangular frame (slot) and a smaller bar with upward projections.\n- Colored pixels inside the slot mark target columns.\n\nRule:\n- Move the bar upward into the slot so its bottom edge sits one cell above the slot's interior bottom.\n- Shift it horizontally so the bar's upward projections align column-wise with the colored pixels in the slot.\n- Stack the colored pixels immediately above the bar projections in each column.\n\nKey insight: The bar's projections act as keys that must align with the colored-column \"locks\" in the slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"db118e2a\" lines=\"7-23\">\n<description>\nNormalize the framed input motif into a 5x5 icon and stamp two copies on a 15x15 canvas.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is a rounded rectangular frame: the four corners are the background\ncolor, and every other outer-border pixel has one frame color. Non-background\npixels inside that frame are the motif to preserve.\n\nRule\nMake a 5x5 version of the rounded frame with a 3x3 interior. Crop the input\ninterior motif to its bounding box, center that crop in the 3x3 interior, and\npaste the resulting 5x5 icon at upper-left positions (2, 2) and (8, 8) in a\n15x15 background canvas.\n\nKey insight\nThe variable-size frame is only a carrier; the meaningful object is the\ncompact interior motif, which is normalized to the fixed 3x3 icon interior.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9bbf930d\" lines=\"61-72\">\n<description>\nMove magenta particles along corridors formed by parallel colored bars.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg=7. Particles=6 in col 0. Colored bars on even rows form corridors on odd rows.\n\nRule:\n- Particle goes straight through bg cells.\n- When blocked by a colored cell, turns 90° into the perpendicular direction\n  that has a bg cell flanked by same-color bars. Stops at grid edge or full blockage.\n\nKey insight: Same-color parallel bars define the only valid corridors, so each particle follows a deterministic local trace.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d968ffd4\" lines=\"15-28\">\n<description>\nExtend each of two aligned rectangular marker objects toward the other, filling half of the gap across the full perpendicular span.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains exactly two solid, same-sized monochromatic rectangles.\n- The rectangles share either the same row span or the same column span, with a background gap between their facing edges.\n\nRule\n- If the rectangles are side-by-side, color the left half of the inter-object gap across every row with the left object's color, and the right half with the right object's color.\n- If one rectangle is above the other, color the upper half of the gap across every column with the upper object's color, and the lower half with the lower object's color.\n- When the gap length is odd, leave the center row/column as background.\n\nKey insight\n- The marker objects are endpoints of an axial corridor; their own size identifies the facing edge, while the filled corridor spans the whole grid perpendicular to that axis.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"66ac4c3b\" lines=\"57-72\">\n<description>\nComplete the sparse-color reflected copy of the foreground motif across its matching marker row or column.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one background color and two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color appears only as a sparse marker row or column.\n- The other foreground color contains the same sparse marker positions on a parallel row or column, plus the motif on the far side of the sparse markers.\n\nRule\n- Keep the original grid unchanged.\n- Reflect every motif pixel of the larger foreground color across the two parallel marker lines.\n- Paint the reflected motif pixels with the sparse marker color.\n\nKey insight\n- The two equal marker rows or columns are paired landmarks: the sparse-color marker line borders the source motif, and the matching source-color marker line anchors the reflected copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8618d23e\" lines=\"9-25\">\n<description>\nStagger the two horizontal input regions onto a larger maroon separator canvas.\n\nInput structure:\n- The input is a rectangular grid with an even number of rows.\n- The top half and bottom half are the two regions to preserve.\n- Maroon is absent from the input and is used only as the new separator color.\n\nRule:\n- Create an output with one extra row and one extra column, filled with maroon.\n- Copy the top half into the upper-left corner.\n- Copy the bottom half one row below the top region and one column to the right.\n\nKey insight:\nThe output is not a recoloring of either region; it inserts an L-shaped maroon\ngap by diagonally offsetting the lower half from the upper half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e576d6e\" lines=\"159-181\">\n<description>\nDraw the marker-colored route through the centers of all doors in the parallel wall maze.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a background, parallel full-span walls, one three-pixel door\n  segment in each wall, and two marker pixels sharing a color.\n- Walls are either all vertical or all horizontal, and the markers are on\n  opposite outer sides of the wall set.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the scene unchanged except for the route.\n- Visit the door centers in wall order from the upper/left marker side to\n  the lower/right marker side.\n- In each corridor, draw an orthogonal path to the cell just before the next\n  wall, move along that wall to the door center, cross the center, and\n  continue to the next corridor; after the last door, connect to the other\n  marker.\n\nKey insight:\n- The three-pixel door segments mark the only crossing points through a set\n  of parallel walls, so the output is the corridor path threaded through\n  those centers.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9968a131\" lines=\"10-27\">\n<description>\nShift alternating two-color row bars so each row aligns to the topmost bar's color columns.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background surrounds horizontal two-pixel bars.\n- Every foreground row has exactly one adjacent bar using the same two colors.\n- The topmost bar gives the reference left/right column for each color.\n\nRule:\n- Keep bars whose left color is already in its reference column.\n- For a reversed bar, shift the whole two-pixel bar horizontally until its\n  left color lands in that color's reference column.\n- Background cells vacated by shifted bars become background.\n\nKey insight:\n- The rows are not recolored; each two-color bar is translated so equal\n  colors line up with the topmost reference bar.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"21897d95\" lines=\"283-301\">\n<description>\nRepaint regions from T-shaped control markers, erase the markers, and optionally rotate based on a color-9 L-marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a mosaic of monochrome regions.\n- Color 1 forms T-shaped control markers on region boundaries.\n- Some controls use three singleton color-1 pixels around a colored center; that center is a literal replacement color.\n- An optional 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker triggers 90-degree rotation of the output.\n\nRule:\n- For a color-1 T marker, the single stem points at the destination region to repaint.\n- The two diagonal cells behind the stem identify the source region; the destination gets that source region's original color.\n- For a colored-center T marker, the same stem identifies the destination, but the center color is the replacement.\n- All marker cells are filled with the final color of the source-side region.\n- A 5-cell L-shaped color-9 marker controls rotation: ┐ (corner top-right) → CCW 90°, ┌ (corner top-left) → CW 90°.\n\nKey insight:\n- The stem chooses what changes, while the two diagonals behind the stem choose where the marker itself belongs.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"252143c9\" lines=\"14-33\">\n<description>\nKeep only the center color and redraw it as a diagonal ray to its indicated corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an odd square with a unique dominant background color.\n- The center pixel is foreground.\n- Every off-center pixel with the center color lies in the same diagonal\n  quadrant relative to the center.\n\nRule:\n- Fill the whole output with the background color.\n- Use the quadrant of the center-colored pixels to choose one diagonal\n  direction from the center.\n- Draw a continuous line of the center color from the center to the grid\n  edge in that direction.\n\nKey insight:\nThe color at the exact center identifies the surviving color, while the\nother pixels of that color identify which corner the center should connect to.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"973e499e\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nExpand each input pixel into a miniature same-color mask of the whole grid.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a non-empty two-dimensional grid of foreground colors.\n- Black is reserved for the empty/background pixels introduced in the expanded output.\n- The output has one input-shaped block for each input pixel.\n\nRule\n- For every input pixel with color c, replace that pixel by a block the same shape as the input.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
