uted round-robin across interior gaps first, then the two outer margins,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f15e1fac.py:16:    Flow teal pixels across the grid while red edge pixels push the paths aside.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f15e1fac.py:85:        # draw across the grid one pixel at a time adjusting for red pixel effects\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/9/code.py:91:#       the ordered palette is explicit and repeated across all samples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/13/code.py:200:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/27/code.py:45:#     - No bg cell is allowed to become the seed color in the output. That makes the swap legible across 2-4 examples:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/3/code.py:19:#    sprite is reached. Background cells still accumulate all shots across the whole cascade; one shot paints 9, two or\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/3/code.py:41:#    axis-fired V sprites and diagonal-fired L sprites stay visible across training samples. This makes the extra\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/3/code.py:47:#    Color semantics stay sparse and repeated across samples. 9 always means either a struck sprite or a singly reached\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/3/code.py:276:    - Across the entire cascade, any background cell reached by one total shot becomes maroon (9), while a background\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/16/code.py:29:  - the marker color is shared across all objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/16/code.py:85:- A human can see, across a few examples, that output-left objects always have corner\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/16/code.py:92:  making the repeated ordering pattern visible across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:11:# To make the output grid, you should place a 3x3 green cross pattern between the two blue pixels exactly halfway between them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:15:    Place a green 3x3 cross centered halfway between two blue pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:29:      the same green cross pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:38:    # Generate the 3x3 green cross pattern between the two blue pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:39:    green_cross_sprite = np.array( [[Color.BLACK, Color.GREEN, Color.BLACK],\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:43:    # Put the cross centered at the midpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:46:    output_grid = paste(output_grid, green_cross_sprite, upper_left_r, upper_left_c, transparent=Color.BLACK, clip=True)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:59:    # Randomly place two blue pixels on the grid and ensure a 3x3 green cross pattern can be placed in the middle of them.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e9614598.py:67:    # Ensure there is enough space for the 3 x 3 green cross pattern and two blue pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/19/code.py:32:#    structural role repeated across samples:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/19/code.py:46:#      defined as 4-neighbor crosses whose full 3x3 neighborhood is not all foreground.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/19/code.py:54:#    compact filled squares turn uniformly orange, while cross shapes split into a teal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/19/code.py:125:    - Exactly one foreground color forms a mixture of clutter, solid 3x3 squares, and thin '+' crosses on the background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/19/code.py:126:    - The '+' targets are visually cross-shaped rather than filled 3x3 patches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/19/code.py:134:    - The solver must separate two 