ckground. Several non-bg colors appear as separate monochrome sprites, each close to a shape symmetric across both a horizontal axis and a vertical axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/19/code.py:43:#      templates use top-band colors from the opposite side, and those templates repeat across many slots.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/14/code.py:224:# ---- Distinct marker colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/88a62173.py:15:    - The middle row and middle column are the background color, forming a separator cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/88a62173.py:16:    - The separator cross divides the grid into four same-sized rectangular quadrant regions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/4/code.py:35:     * one shared row band per index across the two groups\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/4c5c2cf0.py:13:# To make the output, reflect the other object across the axes of reflectional symmetry of the small pattern.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/4c5c2cf0.py:17:    Reflect the non-symmetric object across the horizontal and vertical symmetry axes of the separate symmetric marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/4c5c2cf0.py:35:    # 2. Reflect the non-symmetric object across the axes of symmetry of the symmetric object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/4c5c2cf0.py:66:    # 2. Reflect the non-symmetric object across the axes of symmetry of the symmetric object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/13/code.py:52:#    Each plus-bearing component is built as a 5-cell cross plus a one-cell-wide snake\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/13/code.py:61:#    cannot be explained by top-to-bottom or left-to-right ordering. Across examples,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/13/code.py:72:#    includes at least one duplicated band across disconnected components, which gives\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/13/code.py:167:    - Each target '+' is embedded inside a larger foreground component instead of appearing as an isolated 5-cell cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/28/code.py:22:#    is deleted unless it also has a partner across that whole-object center line.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/28/code.py:36:#    is no cross-object interference.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/28/code.py:92:    - Remove every same-color pixel whose mirror partner across that fixed axis is absent.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/13e47133/12/code.py:304:# ---- Distinct control colors across grid ----\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/46/code.py:96:   - Unique colors are used across all valid objects and distractors so monochromatic component\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/46/code.py:113:   - `order_switch_active`: compact row-sorted stack != compact col-sorted stack\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/46/code.py:424:        \"order_switch_active\": int(not np.array_equal(col_stack, row_stack)),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/46/code.py:519:        if metrics[\"order_switch_active\"] < 1:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/8d5021e8.py:32:    - The output is generated by reflecting the source across the left and horizontal band edges.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/11/code.py:46:#    relative frequency and proximity, which are visible structural cues across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/21/code.py:74:#      This is small enough for humans to track across 2-4 examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f8a8fe49.py:17:    Mirror the inner pattern pieces outward across the two red frame halves.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/f8a8fe49.py:27:    - Mirror each half across its corresponding frame line and paste it outside the frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/13/code.py:42:#      mapping small enough to infer, yet repeated enough across examples that the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/13/code.py:93:#      condition (segment orientation). Across a few examples the solver can infer\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/22/code.py:63:#   - `centers -> cross cells -> square cells` 的三阶段写入假设检测集合固定，不会因为中间写 teal 反过来制造新中心；\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/22/code.py:166:    Key insight: The transformation is a fusion of a global filter and a local multi-step expansion: first select the only component that matters, then infer that each accepted '+' grows from center to cross to full square, while visually identical '+' motifs elsewhere stay untouched.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/22/code.py:190:    cross_mask = center_mask.copy()\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/22/code.py:195:                cross_mask[nr, nc] = True\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/22/code.py:196:    out[cross_mask] = 8\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/22/code.py:199:    square_mask = cross_mask.copy()\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/1f876c06.py:50:            assert int(out[r, c]) in (bg, color), \"diagonal paths must not cross other colors\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/34/code.py:49:#    hole feature visually obvious across samples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/34/code.py:153:    - Whenever a colored pixel lacks its mirror partner across that axis, fill the reflected partner if that cell is background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/37/code.py:453:    - Trace each boundary ray step by step and stop it as soon as the next step would cross the opposite-facing partner's front opening edge instead of running to the canvas edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/28/code.py:112:    \"\"\"Count separator pips across a row/column span.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:11:# In the input you will see some number of colored crosses, each of which is 3 pixels tall, 3 pixels wide, and has a single pixel in the center of the cross that is a different color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:12:# Make the output by growing the cross by 1 pixel north/south/east/west, and growing the center pixel by 2 pixels along each of the 4 diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:16:    Expand each two-color cross into a larger cross-and-diagonal motif.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:19:    - The input contains small 3x3 cross objects on a black background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:20:    - Each cross has one arm color and a different center color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:24:    - Locate the center and bounding box of each cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:33:    # extract the 3x3 crosses\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:34:    crosses = find_components(input_grid, bg=Color.BLACK, connectivity=4, monochromatic=False, return_format=\"objects\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:38:    for cross in crosses:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:40:        _b = bbox(cross != Color.BLACK)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:48:        center_color = cross[center_r, center_c]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:49:        cross_color = cross[cross != Color.BLACK][0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:51:        # grow the cross\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:58:                # grow the cross north/south/east/west\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:60:                    output_grid[output_r, output_c] = cross_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:73:    # create 2 crosses\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:74:    for cross_number in range(2):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:76:        cross = np.zeros((3, 3), dtype=int)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:77:        cross_color, center_color = random.sample(list(Color.NOT_BLACK), 2)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:78:        cross[1, :] = cross_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:79:        cross[:, 1] = cross_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:80:        cross[1, 1] = center_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:83:        _loc = first_free_location(input_grid, cross, transparent=Color.BLACK, border=0, padding=0, padding_connectivity=8)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:88:        # blit the cross to the canvas\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0962bcdd.py:89:        paste(input_grid, cross, r, c, transparent=Color.BLACK, clip=True)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/19/code.py:29:#   0..N-1, unique across frames/sprites, so matching is unambiguous. The generator also\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/19/code.py:52:#   Across a few samples, a solver can see that the large squares have thicker rims, that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/29/code.py:65:#       away, producing a sparse border-only cross anchored to the frame perimeter.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:9:#   bands at db in {1, 4}. Here the two full-length axes cross in the middle of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:15:#       cols 2..5 around a center cross at row 8 / col 10 has db=2 and dr=4, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:34:#   moat around every rectangle, and keeps a 1-cell gap from both cross lines.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:38:#   The full blue and red lines remain visually simple, crossing once at a black\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:40:#   only new reasoning burden is \"measure distances to the center cross\" instead of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:45:#   2. Each line is a full row or column with exactly one marker hole at the cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:134:    blue_orient, blue_idx, blue_cross = _find_line(g, BLUE, MARKER)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:135:    red_orient, red_idx, red_cross = _find_line(g, RED, MARKER)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:138:    if blue_cross != red_cross:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/29/code.py:139:        raise AssertionError(\"blue/red lines must cross at the same marker\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/6/code.py:126:    - One foreground color draws mixed shapes on the bg; some targets are isolated 5-cell crosses and some are crosses embedded in larger fg components.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/6/code.py:133:    Key insight: The detection cue is still the same thin orthogonal cross as in the original task, but the allowed contexts are broader and the output footprint is larger, so both clean isolated crosses and embedded crosses must trigger the same square-filling transform.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/28/code.py:166:    - `type_switches`: number of adjacent type changes in the sorted sequence.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/28/code.py:187:        \"type_switches\": int(sum(seq[i] != seq[i - 1] for i in range(1, len(seq)))),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/36/code.py:254:    - Then, in the still-unclaimed area, use the remaining unique-color anchors to recover the shared-color sprites, and within each recovered sprite delete every pixel whose mirror across that sprite's best vertical axis is not the same color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/16/code.py:25:#     single sample instead of being implied only across a dataset.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:8:    \"\"\"Paint a yellow 3x3 ring around the crossing of the full foreground row and column.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:13:    - Their crossing pixel lies away from the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:17:    - Paint the 3x3 neighborhood centered on their crossing yellow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:18:    - Restore the original crossing pixel, leaving only the eight surrounding cells changed.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:21:    - The row/column crossing is the center of a local marker that replaces both nearby bar pixels and nearby background pixels.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:27:    crossing_rows = np.flatnonzero(foreground.all(axis=1))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:28:    crossing_cols = np.flatnonzero(foreground.all(axis=0))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:29:    assert len(crossing_rows) == 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:30:    assert len(crossing_cols) == 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:32:    r = int(crossing_rows[0])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:33:    c = int(crossing_cols[0])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:44:# Canvas / crossing bounds.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:62:        crossing_color = col_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:66:        crossing_color = row_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/67a423a3.py:71:    expected[row, col] = crossing_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:29:#    fixed across all frames. Example: if the latest frame shows a Right-facing L\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:32:#    In the fused task, a horizontal wall crossing row 4 blocks the upward ray, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:78:#    remain legible across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:706:    cross: tuple[int, int],\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:712:    y, x = cross\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:713:    if cross in blocked:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:787:            cross_positions = _candidate_block_positions(heads[i], heads[j])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:788:            if not cross_positions:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:793:                ((_dist(pos, heads[j]), pos) for pos in cross_positions),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:801:            for cross in picks:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:802:                trial = _propose_segment(rng, cross, dd, h, w, blocked)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:823:            cross_positions = _candidate_block_positions(heads[i], heads[j])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:824:            rng.shuffle(cross_positions)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:825:            for cross in cross_positions[:4]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/23/code.py:826:                seg = _propose_segment(rng, cross, dd, h, w, blocked)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/13/code.py:30:#    - Across 2-4 examples, a person can see two stable cues: separator / background colors vary, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/4/code.py:24:#         connected bg hole region, but the hole contour differs across objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/4/code.py:41:#     - Rectangular outer frames are shared across a sample, keeping the layout learnable\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/4/code.py:61:#     - Across examples a human can see that the output is a grid of rectangles, not squares.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/18/code.py:75:   - Across examples, humans can see that outputs contain only stacked\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/18/code.py:91:   - Decorative color varies across samples, but its structural signature is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/7/code.py:52:#    Across examples, the visible gap between the two wall sprites changes, and both the wedge truncation and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/9ecd008a.py:12:    - The missing patch's reflected counterpart across the vertical axis is fully colored.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:22:#    - New combo task: detect an incomplete cross whose visible part is an exact thin L:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:25:#      and coloring the whole completed cross teal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:36:#    - The generator repeats all four elbow rotations across samples so the human-visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:37:#      rule is \"complete corners into symmetric crosses\", not a one-off orientation trick.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:51:#    - Across examples, the only edited regions are bent 3-cell corners that become symmetric\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:52:#      teal crosses. Two new teal pixels appear exactly in the absent orthogonal directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:139:    - One foreground color (for example yellow) forms thin segments and bends on the bg; the target motifs are 3-cell L-corners, not finished crosses.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/14/code.py:146:    Key insight: The solver must recognize a broken rotationally symmetric cross from its asymmetric L-shaped fragment. Detection is done on the original input only, and each detected elbow is expanded to the unique full '+' centered at the same pixel.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/36/code.py:104:   - Because both frame and solid groups include non-4 examples across the dataset,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/23/code.py:80:#      Across examples, that repeated pairing exposes the embedded-marker lookup channel.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/22/code.py:69:#    A human can infer the rule from repeated cues across samples:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/22/code.py:70:#    - the sprite body alternates between two colors across time snapshots;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/22/code.py:413:    Move a triangular sprite across the grid by color-gated half-line chasing, while keeping a recolored trail.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/25/code.py:50:    - Old D8-only logic assumed the motif center was always the anchored point. That fails once the legend can switch\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/25/code.py:60:    - Across examples, the same corner symbol changes which motif point is treated as the anchor, while block markers\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/12/code.py:66:#      objects from merging and creating accidental cross-component pluses.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:86:    - A human can see three repeated cues across examples:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/28/code.py:103:      count pairing, and multi-side hole recoloring across the sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/178fcbfb.py:29:      horizontally across their rows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/21/code.py:48:#    - Intermediate trail cells are reserved so later sprites cannot occupy or cross\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/21/code.py:77:#      trail crossings.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/26/code.py:60:def _find_cross_axes(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> dict:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/26/code.py:140:    axes = _find_cross_axes(g, bg)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/26/code.py:248:    Reorder rectangles around a centered cross of blue/red lines.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/26/code.py:251:    - Detect a centered cross instead of corner-aligned edge lines.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/22/code.py:52:#    - One-pass independence across colors: broken by the combo. The middle sprite must be settled first so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/26/code.py:35:#    - Tight packing changes the global output silhouette in an obvious repeated way across samples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/26/code.py:89:    \"\"\"Return monochrome connected components across every color present in the grid.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/32/code.py:68:#    Across 2-4 examples a human sees two distinct sparse patterns:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/6/code.py:26:#   the true suffix markers are exactly the ones that persist across all reverse-\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/6/code.py:70:#   more unresolved dots appearing as one scans rightward. Across examples, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/6/code.py:93:#   continuation markers are exactly the intersection of the per-frame marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/6/code.py:370:    - Recover its true pending markers by intersecting marker sets across the full reverse-time strip; those shared dots are the ones still present in every older snapshot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/6/code.py:374:    Key insight: Reverse order plus one injected branch per panel means the left edge is the start state, but only the markers shared across all frames belong to its true continuation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/3345333e.py:26:    - Reflect every remaining object pixel across the detected mirror axes to fill the missing cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/34/code.py:66:   Across a few examples a human can still see the main rule: count how many holes each loose sprite has\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/34/code.py:68:   - the canvas background color varies across samples\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/22/code.py:70:#    - Marker slots are still unique across templates, so at most one stamp owns each output block.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/22/code.py:77:#    - The repeated color across the two legend rows identifies one of the template's source colors, giving\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/31/code.py:59:#    - Across examples, the output always has three segments separated by two full bg rows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/31/code.py:68:#      The same motif is reused across bands so color variation does not compete with the geometric signal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/c1d99e64.py:65:# Foreground density over nonblank row/column intersections.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/14/code.py:31:#      amounts across the visible sequence.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/14/code.py:89:#      color corresponds to the drop in future-marker count across its two frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/14/code.py:336:    - Recover the sprite and count the remaining markers in each visible frame; the count drops across adjacent visible frames reveal how many markers were consumed in each irregular interval.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/30/code.py:30:#       A leftmost sprite has true pixels in cols 2 and 5 that mirror across its axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/30/code.py:50:#     - Axis search across the full input width is still valid because the input canvas\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/30/code.py:66:#     - Across examples, outputs have both more pixels than inputs and a smaller frame\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/30/code.py:92:    - For each non-bg color independently, find the vertical axis that best matches the observed pixels and fill any missing same-color mirror partners across that axis.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/6/code.py:14:#      and rows 3 and 7 are fully separator-colored across the core width. The middle-band markers are still\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/36/code.py:71:# - Across 2-4 examples, a human can spot that paired objects share the same corner marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:51:#      4-connected component, which gives repeated evidence across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:94:#    - Across examples the same wall family appears with different arrow directions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:119:#    - The capped side is contiguous across the selected face; generator explicitly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:272:        assert (0, v) in local_pts, \"expected contiguous cap across the marked side\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/e3497940.py:23:    \"\"\"Fold the grid across its solid vertical separator, overlaying the reflected right side onto the left side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/48/code.py:83:#      practice across all objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/27/code.py:40:# - Each sample contains 4-6 objects split across two bands, preserving the old\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/27/code.py:34:#       are drawn from a separate small palette and intentionally reused across different\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/27/code.py:81:#       motif parts, making the new correspondence rule explicit across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6cdd2623.py:62:            # point on left/right => draw horizontal line across through it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:27:#      cross centered at (8,8) while the rest of both trajectories stay maroon.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:30:#      center of a blue explosion cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:33:#    - The generator always includes at least one genuine mid-flight intersection, not\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:41:#      learner sees both maroon-only trajectories and blue collision crosses together.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:50:#      crosses a sprite, the output would become ambiguous. The combo generator therefore\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:52:#    - Explosion crosses now coexist with trails. Write order matters: maroon trails are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:53:#      painted first, hit sprites next, and blue crosses last so collisions override the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:66:#      non-collision because only shared path cells create a 5-cell blue cross.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:67:#    - The generator forces the key fused cue: at least one blue cross appears at a cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:73:#      messy board full of accidental overlaps and makes the blue cross easy to locate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:76:#      those path cells simply get overwritten by the blue cross in the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:81:#      same background path cell -> blue explosion cross; landing on a sprite -> recolor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:90:#      the generator keeps collision crosses away from all sprite cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:155:def _collision_cross(center: tuple[int, int]) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:160:def _paint_collision_cross(grid: np.ndarray, center: tuple[int, int], color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:162:    for r, c in _collision_cross(center):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:178:    - If two or more projectiles pass through the same background cell anywhere along their paths, that shared cell becomes the center of a blue (1) explosion cross extending one step in the four cardinal directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:227:            _paint_collision_cross(out, cell, COLLISION_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:277:def _cross_is_full_in_bounds(center: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:278:    return all(0 <= r < H and 0 <= c < W for r, c in _collision_cross(center))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:365:    centers = [cell for cell in PLACEMENTS_BY_TRAIL if _cross_is_full_in_bounds(cell)]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:370:        center_footprint = set(_collision_cross(center))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:495:            _paint_collision_cross(out, cell, COLLISION_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:530:            _paint_collision_cross(out, dest, COLLISION_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/4/code.py:569:    reserved_bg.update(_collision_cross(collision_center))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/30/code.py:55:      comes from propagating those seed identities across the full N x N block lattice by nearest distance.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/25/code.py:53:#     These jointly expose inversion, left anchoring, and cross-frame color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/25/code.py:105:#     remapping. The dot color is globally constant across all frames and all\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/23/code.py:50:     never use the sampled input background. This gives repeated support across a few\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/23/code.py:74:   - Across 2-4 examples, a human can see three repeated facts:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/23/code.py:77:       3. the output background and frame interiors switch to a new fixed color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/23/code.py:174:    - rebuild the whole output on canonical background 0, so frame interiors also switch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/8/code.py:73:- Across examples, the input shows repeated rectangle colors in an interleaved pattern\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:13:# In the input you will see a 30x30 grid with at least two rectangles, each with at least one special pixel of a different color, and a crosshair-type pattern outside these rectangles.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:14:# For each of these special pixels, apply the crosshair pattern and extend the points inside the rectangle that special pixel is in, until it reaches the edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:18:    Apply the external crosshair pattern to each special pixel inside the rectangles.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:22:    - The smallest non-background component is a crosshair instruction sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:26:    - Crop the crosshair sprite and infer whether it extends horizontally, vertically, or both.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:27:    - For each rectangle, paste the crosshair centered on every special pixel.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:28:    - Extend the crosshair point color through rectangle-colored cells along the enabled row or column.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:31:    - The small outside crosshair is an instruction object; each special pixel becomes the center\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:43:    # sort the objects by their size in terms of area, the crosshair is the smallest object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:45:    crosshair_object = sorted_objects[0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:48:    # now find the crosshair sprite coordinates\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:49:    _b = bbox(crosshair_object != background_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:51:    crosshair_sprite = crop_bbox(crosshair_object, _b)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:52:    width, height = crosshair_sprite.shape\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:54:    # if the crosshair is wider than it is tall, it extends horizontally, vertically if taller, both if square\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:59:        point_color = crosshair_sprite[0, height // 2]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:62:        point_color = crosshair_sprite[width // 2, 0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:64:        point_color = crosshair_sprite[width // 2, 0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:69:    # for each rectangle, crop it to just the rectangle, find the special pixel, extend the crosshair pattern from it, then add it back to the grid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:87:        # for each special pixel, extend the crosshair pattern\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:89:            # first color the special pixel with the crosshair sprite centered on it\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:90:            cropped_rectangle = paste(cropped_rectangle, crosshair_sprite, r - width // 2, c - height // 2, transparent=background_color, clip=True)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:92:            # then extend the points in the crosshair pattern until they reach the edge of the rectangle\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:118:    # Small crosshair object (smallest component).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:119:    crosshair = np.full((3, 3), background, dtype=int)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:120:    crosshair[1, :] = point_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:121:    crosshair[:, 1] = point_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/264363fd.py:122:    paste(grid, crosshair, 1, 1, transparent=background, clip=False)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/12/code.py:63:#   Across a few samples a human can see two repeated facts: every colored object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/12/code.py:113:    - Reflect every observed same-color pixel across that axis and fill the mirror location when it is still background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/26/code.py:19:#     dots from the right every 2 columns\". Instead, the N dots are spread across\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/26/code.py:147:    Return in-patch columns for N dots spread across the frame interior.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/26/code.py:226:      but widths vary across 7, 9, and 11.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/26/code.py:232:    - Draw that many dots on the frame's middle row, but distribute them evenly across the available interior width:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/26/code.py:295:    Distribute matching sprites across frames with visibility constraints.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/22/code.py:56:       * unique colors across all objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/22/code.py:85:   - Across 2-4 examples, the solver can infer a structural rule:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/14/code.py:43:#    - Each palette pair is placed inside one N-wide tile column on a dedicated palette row; it never crosses a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/14/code.py:45:#    - Marker placements still use unique local slots `(lr, lc)` across templates, so output blocks never have\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/24/code.py:66:#      across logical bands while same-band intervals still overlap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/58f5dbd5/24/code.py:370:    `group_count` is the number of rows/columns across which those band positions vary.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/21/code.py:189:    \"\"\"Distribute matches across frames with capacity 4 and at least one visible diagonal.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/22/code.py:264:      more connected background-hole components. Hole counts are unique across each family.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/27/code.py:62:#      larger area totals, so the draw logic must enumerate 12 legal tally positions across three\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/27/code.py:86:#    - Interior dot positions are regular and right-aligned across all rows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/41/code.py:41:#      across all objects, and distinct rows inside each type. More solids than frames ensures the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/41/code.py:44:#      destination slots, and cross-column color transport.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/41/code.py:59:#    - Unique colors make the color swap traceable across columns.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/41/code.py:63:#      the examples show a real cross-column mapping rather than a trivial recolor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/a5313dff.py:56:      escape to the outer boundary without crossing a red wall.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/8/code.py:35:#      the full interior, so the combo solver switches to an exact hollow-rectangle test on the input.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/8/code.py:48:#      and higher counts spread across the whole interior rather than hugging the right edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/8/code.py:172:      1 dot goes in the interior center, and 2+ dots are spread evenly across the frame's inner width.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/22/code.py:81:#     conditional rule with repeated support across samples, not a one-off case.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/6/code.py:49:#    - Colors merely identify objects. The only cross-object relation is spatial arrangement of holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/4/code.py:41:#      growth. This keeps the visual grammar compact and repeated across examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/4/code.py:76:#    - Accent colors are drawn from only two non-frame colors reused across many sprites.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/4/code.py:256:    - Color matching alone is no longer sufficient: a sprite must first be grouped across\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:17:#      so the output is a genuinely cross-color polyline rather than two monochrome\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:35:#      shadows or single-pixel distractors. Across examples the observer repeatedly sees\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:43:#      \"cross-color\" nature would be visually weak. The generator alternates the two\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:51:#      path across two colors, rotating the order changes the object. We replace it with\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:69:#      cross-pair paths or shadow anchors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:76:#      inferable by varying the active palette across examples while always pairing the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:84:#      and wide empty space between them. This keeps the cross-color polylines readable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:86:#      they remain local cues rather than creating clutter across the whole patch.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:365:    Build cross-color row-major polylines from sequentially paired drawable colors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:373:      across the two colors and intended to interleave along one visible path. Optional\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/35ab12c3/18/code.py:432:SHADOW_COUNT_RANGE = (0, 3)  # 0-2 shadows across the two objects\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/3/code.py:492:    cross_priority_inversions = _pairwise_inversions(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/3/code.py:511:        \"cross_priority_inversions\": int(cross_priority_inversions),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/3/code.py:579:        \"cross_priority_inversions\": _pairwise_inversions(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/291dc1e1/3/code.py:598:    if metrics[\"cross_priority_inversions\"] <= 0 or metrics[\"same_priority_area_inversions\"] <= 0:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2ba387bc/30/code.py:58:#    - Across a few examples, the output always keeps only 4x4 frames/solids while\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/40853293.py:49:    - When a horizontal segment and a vertical segment cross, keep the vertical segment's color at the crossing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/40853293.py:52:    - The sparse input pixels are endpoints of colored line segments; segment orientation, not color id order, resolves crossings.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/40853293.py:78:# Segment count, orientation mix,